  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Facilitating tiger population recovery through threat mitigation at landscape level in Eastern Ghats of Andhra Pradesh, India</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A recent assessment of tiger populations in the Eastern Ghats of Andhra Pradesh, helped identify new corridors, with tigers presumably dispersing from breeding populations within Nagarjunasagar Srisailam tiger reserve (NSTR) and Gundla Brameshwaram Wildlife Sanctuary (GBM). There is an urgent need to secure newly identified landscapes south of GBM where tigers likely disperse from NSTR and GBM. Therefore, it is essential to identify critical dispersal corridors and intensify tiger monitoring in the new landscapes. The project aims to increase tiger densities at NSTR and ensure survival of tigers in the immediate corridors outside designated tiger reserves. Project objectives are: (1) To identify and map threats to tigers and prey species; (2) Mitigate threats by involving government agencies and local communities in the tiger landscape of the Eastern Ghats; (3) Aid in consolidating tiger habitats within tiger reserves by promoting government schemes; and, (4) Provide scientific and conservation support to the Andhra Pradesh Forest Department.</narrative>
  </description>
